1
00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:13,853
Hello, I am Abdul Manan Khan. This is a short demo of our paper on trustworthy onboard vision language damage assessment for multi UAV networks, with perception aware coordination guarantees.

2
00:00:14,253 --> 00:00:35,832
After a disaster, UAVs that restore connectivity can also map building damage. The problem is that off the shelf vision language models grade damage poorly, and coordination guarantees assume we know the objective exactly. One wrong reading can quietly void the guarantee. We set out to close that gap.

3
00:00:36,232 --> 00:00:58,920
On each UAV, every camera frame goes to our fine tuned Qwen 2.5 VL 3B model. We turn its answer into a calibrated belief and a conformal label set with ninety percent coverage. The planner uses the midpoint of each set, and relays its decisions to the other UAVs over the backhaul.

4
00:00:59,320 --> 00:01:20,004
Fine tuning raised damage macro F1 from 0.27 to 0.70. After calibration, the expected calibration error is 0.046, and our label sets cover 93 percent of test images, with a lower bound of 90.6 percent.

5
00:01:20,404 --> 00:01:35,167
Our main result is this bound. A plan computed on perceived weights keeps beta times the optimum, minus a term that counts weight errors only on the users the plans serve, and the conformal sets bound that term.

6
00:01:35,567 --> 00:01:57,854
We also show that relaying the decision list keeps the one half guarantee on any connected backhaul, while one hop coordination can fall to one over k. On our test bench with 200 metre links, the relayed planner leads by 2.3 points at six UAVs; with complete links the one hop baseline is slightly better.

7
00:01:58,254 --> 00:02:16,616
On an exact optimum test bench, planning on the conformal sets instead of the top label halved the loss caused by perception: 1.1 versus 2.1 points of the optimum. In our city scenario, damage barely changed the plan, so we do not claim this gain in flight.

8
00:02:17,016 --> 00:02:40,899
In a network simulator, we let the UAV with the largest gain decide next. This best first relaying beats one hop coordination by 2 percent at eight UAVs and by 25 percent at sixteen UAVs on 200 metre links, while sending fewer messages. Its weak point is that some UAVs lose the gateway path for longer.

9
00:02:41,299 --> 00:03:04,184
Here is a replay of one AirSim City Environ mission, flown by PX4 software autopilots. The model read every downward frame live on a cloud GPU. In 48 missions we saw no collision, and no false major or total damage alarm in 9,734 frames of the intact city.

10
00:03:04,584 --> 00:03:27,701
Finally, we closed the loop on flight hardware. In 12 hardware in the loop missions, a Pixhawk 6C flew one UAV through AirSim, while the Jetson Orin Nano ran both the model and the fleet decisions. There was no failsafe, and the model answered every frame in about 2.2 seconds, inside the five second frame period.

11
00:03:28,101 --> 00:03:49,355
To sum up: a compact vision language model can be a trustworthy input to UAV fleet coordination, when its uncertainty is calibrated and carried into the guarantee. Every test was pre registered, and we report the ones that failed. The code, results and videos are public. Thank you for watching.
